from: reviewer (ta-model-level-demand)
session: §4a enforcement gap (root-cause) + reviewer "highlight changes" default

This handoff carries two governance items for the planner to fold into the backlog and
(where owned) into the conventions docs. Neither is code work; PR C itself is closed out
(see `plan__ta-model-level-demand-review-r4.md`). Dean directed both on 2026-07-29.

## Item 1 — §4a leaks: root-cause of why they were neither prevented nor caught

PR C shipped 14 §4a plans-branch-identifier leaks in code comments / test descriptions,
introduced across two logic commits (`55b0507f`, `b0257e59`) and only found on a manual
round-3 re-scan. Root cause has two independent halves:

**Why the coder created them (prevention failure).** §4a is violated by *transcription*.
The coder worked from the Type 3 plan and this Type 6 review — both of which correctly and
freely use plans-branch identifiers on every line (`decision #1`, `§Tests 1-4`, `§Deferred`,
`TA-demand §3.3/§3.5`, `TA-supply §5.5`, `F1`). When writing a code comment or test
description explaining *why* a line exists, the coder copied the doc sentence with its
identifier intact. `// per review finding F1` is the purest case — the coder was literally
fixing finding F1 and labelled the fix with the review's own finding number. §4a's own text
predicts exactly this ("when you transcribe content into code, strip the identifier"), but
it is a vigilance rule with no gate: the code compiles, passes gofmt/test/lint/DCO/build, and
golangci-lint cannot see a prose-convention violation.

**Why the process didn't catch them (detection failure), at three stages, all eyeballs:**
1. Coder pre-push checklist (CONVENTIONS §Pre-push) = gofmt / test / lint / DCO / build. **No
   §4a step.** Lint can't catch it (project prose convention, not a Go rule).
2. Reviewer rounds 1–2 (mine) — my §4a check scanned only the **dev-guide doc text**, not code
   comments or `It(...)`/`Describe(...)` strings. Gap I own.
3. Reviewer round-3 grep was itself too narrow — matched `review finding F1` but not bare `F1`
   or the `§`/`plan §` family, so I reported **7** sites; the coder's full working-tree re-scan
   found **14** (+2 pre-existing upstream, correctly left out of scope per Dean's "leaks in main
   handled later").

**Through-line:** §4a is a "must-not" enforced entirely by attention, and attention failed at
every layer independently. This is the fourth convention slip in PR C's history to surface only
on manual re-derivation (F1 formula-fork, then §4a across two review rounds).

**Concrete remedy (single mechanical gate that catches all of it):** grep the diff's added
comment/test lines for
`decision #|Decision #|review finding|\bF[0-9]\b|plan §|TA-[a-z]+ §|planning/|-plan\.md|-review\.md`
Add it (a) to the pre-push checklist in CONVENTIONS.md (coder side), and (b) to the reviewer
checklist (reviewer side) — the reviewer grep must cover code comments and `It`/`Describe`
strings, not just dev-guide text. Ownership sits with the still-open "who edits CONVENTIONS.md"
question in the existing governance backlog item; this reinforces that item rather than opening
a new thread. Related memories: `feedback_semantic_pivot_grep` (same "planner-includes-a-grep-
step" structure), `feedback_doc_accuracy_discipline`.

## Item 2 — make "highlight all changes + critical sections" a reviewer default (Dean's request)

Dean liked the round-4 close-out format — a highlight of *all* the changes (per-commit table:
what each commit does + logic-vs-comment-vs-doc classification) plus the critical shipping code
section shown inline — and wants it to be the **default deliverable for internal code reviewers**,
not an ad-hoc one-off. Requested 2026-07-29: "I like the highlight — this should be default for
internal code reviewers — add to handoff for planner to correct."

Planner action: fold this into the reviewer conventions. There is no `REVIEWER-CONVENTIONS.md`
yet (it's candidate direction (2) in the existing governance item); this is a second concrete
requirement for it, alongside the pre-action checklist mirror. Proposed shape of the default,
for whoever authors it:
- **Change highlight** — a per-commit table (commit → one-line what → type: logic / comment-only
  / doc), plus an explicit list of anything deliberately left out of scope and why.
- **Critical section** — the key shipping code shown inline (the smallest region that carries the
  PR's actual behavior), so the reviewer's verdict is legible without re-opening the diff.
- Applies to the internal plan-vs-diff review close-out (the FINAL / ready-to-push summary), not
  every interim round.

Until REVIEWER-CONVENTIONS.md exists, capturing this as a backlog line under the governance item
(candidate direction (2)) is enough — it just needs to not be lost.

